    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im running an organic kale farm out in the pacific northwest and im seeing some yellowing on the lower leaves like a nitrogen deficiency. soil ph is sitting at 6.2 right now and i just put down some compost a few weeks back. should i go with bone meal or blood meal to bump up the nitrogen without messing up my ph too much?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Given your current soil pH of 6.2, which is quite good for many crops, a blood meal is generally the faster and more direct way to boost nitrogen levels for your kale. Bone meal is primarily a source of phosphorus and calcium and will provide a slower, more gradual release of nitrogen. Since you are aiming to address an immediate yellowing issue associated with nitrogen, the blood meal should give you quicker results without drastically shifting your ideal soil pH.",
      "source": "target",
